  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)+1$ is obtained by translating the graph of $y=f(x)-3$ by $4$ units *up.* \n\nHence, if the point $(2,-1)$ lies on $y=f(x)-3$, then the point \n\n$$\n(2,-1+4)=(2,3)\n$$\n\nlies on $y=f(x)+1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228870/assets/468f1e720d1a4834f1547b0cd2fcb8c9bff1e0342d2d98e33a62bbf9d473701a.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAs $(2,-1)$ lies on $y=f(x)-3$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2)-3 & =-1 \\\\ f(2) & =-1+3 \\\\ f(2) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $x=2$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(2)+1 \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(2,3)$ must lie on $y=f(x)+1.$",
